\": 1,\n  1173→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1174→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1175→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1176→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1177→    },\n  1178→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1179→      \"plot_summary\": \"Amontillado recounts the chilling tale of his meticulously planned revenge against Fortunato, a man who has inflicted an unspecified but deeply wounding insult upon him. Set during the chaotic, festive atmosphere of carnival season, Amontillado exploits Fortunato’s pride and love of fine wine by luring him into the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo under the pretense of tasting a rare pipe of Amontillado. Fortunato, clad in a jingling motley and conical cap, is drunk and oblivious, his vanity and masonic pride making him an easy target. As they descend deeper into the vaults, Amontillado feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, offering wine to keep him compliant, while subtly revealing his family’s coat of arms and motto, 'Nemo me impune lacessit,' foreshadowing his intent to punish without consequence.\\n\\nThe tension escalates as Amontillado’s true intentions become clear. When Fortunato boasts of his masonic affiliation, Amontillado produces a trowel—symbolizing the tools of masonry—and reveals his deadly purpose. He chains Fortunato to a niche in the deepest crypt, where the victim’s cries grow increasingly desperate and his laughter turns to chilling disbelief. Amontillado methodically builds a wall of stone and mortar, sealing Fortunato inside, all while ignoring the pleas and final, drunken laughter that echo through the catacombs. The act is carried out with cold precision, reflecting Amontillado’s obsession with vengeance and his belief in absolute impunity.\\n\\nThe story concludes with Amontillado reflecting on the aftermath: the wall remains intact, undisturbed for fifty years, a testament to the success of his plan. He claims his heart grew sick not from guilt, but from the dampness of the catacombs, underscoring his emotional detachment and the depth of his psychological control. The final image of the jingle of bells—Fortunato’s last audible trace—lingers as a haunting reminder of the man he destroyed. Amontillado’s narrative, delivered in a calm, retrospective tone, reveals a man consumed by a singular, obsessive mission, where identity is defined by revenge, and the past is buried beneath layers of stone and silence.\",\n  1180→      \"themes\": [\n  1181→        \"revenge\",\n  1182→        \"identity\",\n  1183→        \"madness\"\n  1184→      ],\n  1185→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1186→    },\n  1187→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1188→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1189→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1190→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1191→      },\n  1192→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1193→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1194→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1195→      },\n  1196→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1197→        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n  1198→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1199→      },\n  1200→      \"Character Profiles\": {\n  1201→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1202→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1203→      },\n  1204→      \"Pronunciation Guide\": {\n  1205→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n  1206→        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n  1207→      }\n  1208→    },\n  1209→    \"timing\": {\n  1210→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1211→        \"duration_seconds\": 57.83,\n  1212→        \"duration_formatted\": \"57.8s\"\n  1213→      },\n  1214→      \"Character Extraction\": {\n  1215→        \"duration_seconds\": 250.75,\n  1216→        \"duration_formatted\": \"4m 11s\"\n  1217→      },\n  1218→      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n  1219→        \"duration_seconds\": 85.58,\n  1220→        \"duration_formatted\": \"1m 26s\"\n