ence\": \"medium\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Montresor\",\n        \"Fortunato\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolved to punish him with impunity. During the chaotic carnival season at dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—into the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo under the pretense of tasting a rare pipe of Amontillado. As they descend through the vaults, Montresor feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, offering wine to keep him compliant, while subtly revealing his family’s coat of arms and motto, 'Nemo me impune lacessit.' When Fortunato boasts of his masonic affiliation, Montresor produces a trowel, revealing his true intent. He chains Fortunato to a niche in the deepest crypt, then methodically walls him in with stone and mortar, ignoring his increasingly desperate cries and finally his chilling, drunken laughter. After sealing the final stone, Montresor hears only the jingle of bells, and claims his heart grew sick from the catacomb’s dampness, not guilt. The wall remains undisturbed for fifty years.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"characters\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"char_amontillado_1b7d65f7\",\n      \"canonical_name\": \"Amontillado\",\n      \"aliases\": [],\n      \"role\": null,\n      \"appearance\": null,\n      \"personality\": null,\n      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n      \"descriptions\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"The narrator is a first-person storyteller recounting a past event involving a man named Fortunato. He presents himself as someone who orchestrates a carefully planned encounter under the pretense of verifying a wine purchase, while subtly revealing his family's motto and using psychological manipulation to lure Fortunato into a vault. His actions suggest a calculated and deliberate nature, though the text does not confirm his motivations or emotional state beyond the narrative frame.\",\n          \"source_position\": 2046,\n          \"chapter_index\": null,\n          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"relationships\": {},\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"The narrator is the storyteller of the event\",\n          \"quote\": \"The thousand injuries of Fortunato I had borne as I best could, but when he ventured upon insult I vowed revenge.\",\n          \"position\": 2100,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"He fabricates a need to verify a wine purchase\",\n          \"quote\": \"I have received a pipe of what passes for Amontillado, and I have my doubts.\",\n          \"position\": 2100,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"He uses Fortunato's pride in wine expertise against him\",\n          \"quote\": \"And as for Luchresi, he cannot distinguish Sherry from Amontillado.\",\n          \"position\": 2446,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"He subtly reveals his family's coat of arms and motto\",\n          \"quote\": \"I was silly enough to pay the full Amontillado price without consulting you in the matter. You were not to be found, and I was fearful of losing a bargain.\",\n          \"position\": 2365,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"He feigns concern for Fortunato's health while leading him deeper into the vaults\",\n          \"quote\": \"The vaults are insufferably damp. They are encrusted with nitre.\",\n          \"position\": 3108,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"statement\": \"He continues the charade even as Fortunato is trapped\",\n          \"quote\": \"The Amontillado!\",\n          \"position\": 9594,\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        },\n  