arms and motto, 'Nemo me impune lacessit,' foreshadowing his intent to punish without consequence.\\n\\nThe tension escalates as Amontillado’s true intentions become clear. When Fortunato boasts of his masonic affiliation, Amontillado produces a trowel—symbolizing the tools of masonry—and reveals his deadly purpose. He chains Fortunato to a niche in the deepest crypt, where the victim’s cries grow increasingly desperate and his laughter turns to chilling disbelief. Amontillado methodically builds a wall of stone and mortar, sealing Fortunato inside, all while ignoring the pleas and final, drunken laughter that echo through the catacombs. The act is carried out with cold precision, reflecting Amontillado’s obsession with vengeance and his belief in absolute impunity.\\n\\nThe story concludes with Amontillado reflecting on the aftermath: the wall remains intact, undisturbed for fifty years, a testament to the success of his plan. He claims his heart grew sick not from guilt, but from the dampness of the catacombs, underscoring his emotional detachment and the depth of his psychological control. The final image of the jingle of bells—Fortunato’s last audible trace—lingers as a haunting reminder of the man he destroyed. Amontillado’s narrative, delivered in a calm, retrospective tone, reveals a man consumed by a singular, obsessive mission, where identity is defined by revenge, and the past is buried beneath layers of stone and silence.\",\n      \"themes\": [\n        \"revenge\",\n        \"identity\",\n        \"madness\"\n      ],\n      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n    },\n    \"model_usage\": {\n      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-instruct\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Character Extraction\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n        \"provider\": \"ollama\"\n      },\n      \"Chapter Summaries\": {\n        \"model\": \"qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0\",\n    