plicit relationships detected.</p>\n                \n            </section>\n\n            \n        </div>\n\n        <div id=\"chapters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n        <section>\n            <h2>📑 Chapter Guide</h2>\n\n            \n\n            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">2,354 words &middot; 15m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolved to punish him with impunity. During the chaotic carnival season at dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—into the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo under the pretense of tasting a rare pipe of Amontillado. As they descend through the vaults, Montresor feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, offering wine to keep him compliant, while subtly revealing his family’s coat of arms and motto, 'Nemo me impune lacessit.' When Fortunato boasts of his masonic affiliation, Montresor produces a trowel, revealing his true intent. He chains Fortunato to a niche in the deepest crypt, then methodically walls him in with stone and mortar, ignoring his increasingly desperate cries and finally his chilling, drunken laughter. After sealing the final stone, Montresor hears only the jingle of bells, and claims his heart grew sick from the catacomb’s dampness, not guilt. The wall remains undisturbed for fifty years.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                